The {{nihongo|Sanese people|三國人|Sangokujin}} are an ethnic group native to the Sanese archipelago, which is politically divided between the central state of [[Okaiken]], the southern state of [[Nakamizu]], and the northern nation of [[Yanbaru]]. Ethnic Sanese comprise three major cultural subgroups: the Yoroi of Okaiken, the Nakamians of Nakamizu, and the Yanbaru people. The Yoroi make up 77.3% of the total population of Okaiken, alongside significant Kwangju and Atsu minorities, while Nakamians constitute roughly 95% of the population of Nakamizu. The Yanbaru people form the overwhelming majority of the 36 million citizens in the northern island nation of Yanbaru.

Worldwide, approximately 226 million people are of Sanese descent; of these, the vast majority reside within the borders of Nakamizu, Okaiken, and Yanbaru. People of Sanese ancestry who live in other countries across [[Fosia]], [[Lurandia]], or [[Abyala]] are referred to as {{nihongo|sankeijin|三系人}}. The term ethnic Sanese may also be used in some contexts to refer to a locus of ethnic groups including the majority {{nihongo|Yoroi|鎧人|Yoroijin}} and the indigenous Atsu.

==History==
{{Main|History of Okaiken|History of Nakamizu|History of Yanbaru}}

===Theories of origins===
{{Main|Genetic and anthropometric studies on Sanese people}}

Archaeological evidence indicates that Stone Age people lived in the Sanese archipelago during the Paleolithic period between 39,000 and 21,000 years ago. The archipelago was then connected to mainland Fosia by at least one land bridge, where nomadic hunter-gatherers crossed into the region. Flint tools and bony implements of this era have been excavated in northern Harujima and the Yanbaru islands.

The origins of the Sanese people are traced back to a complex mixture of ancient hunter-gatherers native to the archipelago, categorized by archaeologists as the Harujima-Kaze culture, and successive waves of agricultural migrants from the neighboring continental shores. As the climate stabilized around 5,000 BC, the Oita-Yagi horizon emerged, marking the first permanent human habitation of the southern islands. This period saw the introduction of wet-field farming and primitive livestock management, technologies believed to have been brought by seafaring migrants from the Indus region.

The indigenous Atsu, a distinct ethnic group whose origins are traced to a synthesis of Sukoyrian maritime and Mundolian steppe cultures, had populated the majority of the archipelago by the 2nd millennium BC. However, the arrival of warlike tribes from the Indus basin initiated a centuries-long period of conflict, eventually forcing the Atsu lineages into the mountainous southern and western reaches of Yagikoshu.

Currently, the most well-regarded theory is that present-day Sanese people formed from both the Indus rice-agriculturalists and the various indigenous hunter-gatherer ethnicities, creating a highly resilient, agrarian, and maritime society adapted to the inverted and often severe geography of their island chain.

===Classical and Feudal periods===
{{Main|Maki period|Asama period|Heiji period|Sengoku Period (Okaiken)}}

Beginning around 300 BC, the early Sanese states began to consolidate. The Maki period (45 BC – 10 AD) represents the maturation of the Mitoyagi state into a centralized administrative power, centered at the majestic court of Meiji-kyō. This era is distinguished by the Code of Fiefs, transitioning the realm from a tribal union into a formal empire.

The Asama period (710–794 AD) saw the zenith of the early Sanese state, defined by the definitive transition to a highly centralized, bureaucratic empire mirroring the administrative rigor of the Zong dynasties. The cultural flowering of Asama was fueled by a unique spiritual tension between the native Shoto faith and the arrival of new philosophies from Kwangju and Zong.

The broader Sanese ethnic identity remained culturally unified until the political rupture of the [[Kitayama Period]] in the 14th century. The Schism of the Dual Suns sparked a brutal succession war that split the Imperial line, creating a Minato-backed Northern Court in Mito (modern Okaiken) and a Southern Court in Miyagami (modern Nakamizu). This permanent partition allowed the central and southern Sanese populations to develop distinct societal structures. Following the outbreak of the Taiju War in 1467, the northern domains of Okaiken were plunged into the [[Sengoku Period]], a century of near-constant military conflict and social upheaval. This bloodshed only concluded when the "Three Unifiers" forced the nation into a rigid, isolationist peace under the Honda Shogunate. 

Conversely, the south experienced its own localized fragmentation known as the Sengoku Ransei, but later embraced a more cosmopolitan and open society during the Inaba Period under the Kawaei clan, engaging in massive invasions into the Fosian mainland. Meanwhile, the Yanbaru people in the northern archipelago established a distinct maritime kingdom. They enjoyed a long, peaceful era shielded from the violence of the Sengoku periods, acting as a critical tributary and trade intermediary with the Zong Empire and the coastal states of Fosia.

===Imperial and Modern periods===
{{See also|Saito Restoration|First Empire of Nakamizu}}

During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, the central state of Okaiken underwent rapid modernization during the Saito Restoration of 1871. During this Imperial expansionist period, the phrase Sanese people was used to refer not only to residents of the archipelago, but also to people from territories who held Imperial citizenship, such as those in Kwangju and occupied parts of Zong. Such linguistic distinctions facilitated forced cultural assimilation of colonized ethnic identities into a single Imperial identity.

After the end of the Great Adonian War in 1952, Okaiken was stripped of its overseas territories and underwent a transformation through the Quiet Revolution, eventually landing on the modern Jutsu Restoration in 2011. Nakamizu followed its own trajectory of industrialization and democratization, maintaining a slightly more diverse demographic makeup due to its historically open borders with the Fosian mainland. Yanbaru, having navigated the colonial era as a fiercely independent maritime hub, modernized its own economy while strictly preserving its unique indigenous identity.

==Language==
{{Main|Sanese language|Nakamian dialect|Yanbaru languages}}

The [[Sanese language]] family is a Minic language group treated as a language isolate; it is also related to the Ogasawara languages, and both are sometimes suggested to be part of the proposed Pan-Aisaic language family. The mainstream language utilizes a tripartite writing system using Hiragana, Katakana, and Kanji.

Domestic Sanese people use primarily Sanese for daily interaction, though linguistic policies diverge wildly between the three nations. In the south, the Nakamian dialect has incorporated diverse loanwords due to Nakamizu's historically open borders. In the central state of Okaiken, the Ministry of Education enforces a strict bilingual mandate known as the STEP REP Program. This ensures that the younger generation of Sanese possesses a near-native fluency rate in Mercian (English), designed to cultivate a globally competitive technocrat class. 

To the north, the Yanbaru languages are spoken exclusively within the nation of Yanbaru. While closely related to standard Sanese, they are mutually unintelligible to Yoroi or Nakamian speakers, preserving archaic phonetic features and complex vowel systems that were lost in the central and southern islands centuries ago. The adult literacy rate across all three nations exceeds 99%.

==Religion==
{{Main|Religion in Okaiken|Religion in Nakamizu|Religion in Yanbaru}}

The spiritual life of the Sanese people is characterized by a deeply ingrained syncretism, though the theological expression of this faith varies significantly by nation. It generally blends the philosophical and soteriological elements of continental Baiduism (predominantly Pure Land Baiduism and the austere Sen Buddhism) with native shamanic traditions, most notably Triskaidetheism and animistic nature worship known broadly as Shoto.

In Okaiken and Nakamizu, religious institutions were historically integrated into the state apparatus. During the Honda Shogunate, Buddhist temples served as census bureaus, merging civic duty with spiritual obligation. Today, this fusion is highly visible in religious observances that coexist with ancient astronomical events, such as the theological reverence for the gas giant Quetzal during the quadrennial Day of the Dragon. 

In the northern nation of Yanbaru, the spiritual landscape is dominated by an indigenous, matrifocal animism. Female priestesses govern sacred groves and maintain the spiritual purity of the community. This unbroken religious tradition remains highly distinct from the heavily institutionalized, patriarchal Buddhism of the central and southern states.

==Culture and Character==
{{Main|Culture of Okaiken|Culture of Yanbaru}}

Culturally, the Sanese differ significantly from their neighbors in Shayden or Kwangju. The concept of {{nihongo|Masugu|真直ぐ|Masugu}} (Straight or Direct) is universally valued over ambiguity. In Sanese business and social life, being brutally honest is often seen as a sign of integrity and strength, whereas excessive politeness is viewed with suspicion or interpreted as weakness.

Because the eastern coastlines of the archipelago are frequently battered by massive cyclonic systems known as the Eastern Typhoons, and the entire region is periodically subjected to extreme gravitational king tides, Sanese culture is heavily defined by an ethos of communal endurance. This environmental adversity forged a cultural trait known as ludic resilience, wherein the population rapidly adapts to meteorological disruptions through spontaneous community cooperation and indoor festivals.

Physicality is a core component of central Sanese identity, heavily influenced by the violent history of the Sengoku Period. In modern Okaiken, this manifests as the Cult of Iron. Stemming from mandatory physical education reforms, maintaining a highly muscular and functional physique is considered a civic duty, leading to the massive popularity of full-contact combat sports such as Rugby, Kenka-Jutsu (bare-knuckle striking), and Goriki (Sanese Strongman). In Yanbaru, the culture leans heavily into maritime aesthetics, featuring distinct musical traditions utilizing native stringed instruments, vibrant textile weaving, and architecture defined by heavy stone walls and red-tiled roofs adorned with protective guardian beast statues.

[[Sanese cuisine]] is heavily influenced by the archipelago's maritime history and the rigorous demands of its working class. Unlike the milder diets of neighboring regions, Yoroi and Nakamian food frequently utilizes heavy spices, numbing peppercorns, and indigenous dried chilies to preserve food and provide warmth. In the Sanese lexicon, the word karai refers exclusively to spicy heat, completely distinct from saltiness. Dishes like Mara Gyubara Nabe, a fiercely spicy beef brisket stew eaten with unseasoned short-grain rice, perfectly encapsulate the hearty, utilitarian flavor profiles favored by the southern populations. Yanbaru cuisine, lacking the extreme cold weather of the south, relies more heavily on slow-cooked pork, indigenous root vegetables, and complex coastal broths.

==Literature and Arts==
{{Main|Sanese literature|Sanese art}}

The foundational texts of Sanese literature are defined by a distinct preference for the grand epic. The earliest works, including the Kojiki and Sanshoki chronicles, are sprawling mythological sagas that reflect the Indus influence on the Sanese archipelago. Modern literature in Okaiken was heavily shaped by the Republic Era, giving birth to the Brutalist Realism movement, which depicted survival and endurance in gritty, unromanticized terms.

Sanese sculpture and painting represent the foundational pillars of the nation's artistic heritage. The Sengoku and Honda periods saw the rise of a distinct style known as Kurayami-e or Shadow Pictures. This movement blended flat composition with a visceral, psychological realism.

The fusion of traditional ink-wash techniques and this darker thematic lineage led directly to the creation of manga and anime. Sanese visual media retains a stark, high-contrast aesthetic that favors heavy shadows, grittier character designs, and unromanticized combat narratives, reflecting the cynicism and resilience of the post-Republic populace. In contemporary music, the youth culture is heavily defined by Sanese drill, a localized, aggressive subgenre of hip-hop featuring rapid-fire flows and gritty lyrical themes.

==Citizenship==
The governments of Okaiken, Nakamizu, and Yanbaru consider all naturalized and native-born nationals with a multi-ethnic background as Sanese. In recent decades, to combat a shrinking workforce, Okaiken implemented a controversial Skill-Based Residency program, accepting new naturalized citizens annually and absorbing significant Kwangju and Zong populations into the Sanese demographic. Nakamizu, having maintained historically open borders during the Inaba Period, possesses a slightly higher baseline of demographic integration.

Children born to international couples receive Sanese nationality when one parent is a national. However, Sanese law in Okaiken states that children who hold dual citizenship must choose one nationality upon reaching adulthood.

==Diaspora==
{{Main|Sanese diaspora}}

The term {{nihongo|sankeijin|三系人|sankeijin}} is used to refer to Sanese people who emigrated from the archipelago and their descendants.

Emigration from the Sanese islands became a mass phenomenon during the late 19th century and mid-20th century, particularly driven by trade agreements and the search for natural resources. Large Sanese communities exist in the Republic of Zong, Shayden, and the Kwangju territories. In the New World continents of Lurandia and Abyala, historic diasporas were established following early colonial expeditions by Fosian powers.

The most significant modern diaspora is found in the Confederation of Balisca, fueled by the modern Lithium Security Pact. Sanese Baliscans ({{nihongo|Sangokujin Barisuka-jin|三國人バリスカ人}}) consist of Baliscan citizens who are nationals or naturals of Sanese ancestry, as well as Sanese immigrants living in Balisca. In 2024, the Instituto Confederal de Stadistica stated that there were over 2.6 million Sanese descendants in Balisca, representing roughly 1.7% of the total Baliscan population. This makes it the country with the largest population of Sanese origin outside of East Fosia. Sanese Baliscans predominantly practice Mahayana Buddhism and Shotoism, speak both Baliscano and Sanese, and actively participate in syncretic cultural festivals throughout the Baliscan archipelago alongside other Sankeijin and Atsu diaspora groups.

